# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Root .env — the ONLY file `docker compose` reads.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# DO NOT `cp` this file. Run `npm run env:init`, which copies it to `.env` and
# fills in a fresh random value for every secret left empty below. Copying it by
# hand gives you a file full of empty passwords, and Compose will refuse to
# start until you have filled every one of them in yourself.
#
# This file exists because its absence caused a real outage. Compose resolved
# `${REDIS_PASSWORD:-<a default>}` from THIS file — it never reads
# apps/api/.env, which is what the services themselves load. With no root .env,
# Compose silently used the fallback while the services sent the password from
# apps/api/.env, and every Redis call failed with WRONGPASS.
#
# The failure was invisible for a second reason worth knowing:
#
#   * Postgres writes its password into the data directory at first init, so it
#     kept accepting the old password from its persisted volume and looked fine.
#   * Redis takes `--requirepass` from the command line on EVERY start, so it
#     adopted the fallback the moment the container was recreated.
#
# Same missing file, one service unaffected and one broken — which is why this
# looked like a Redis bug rather than a configuration one.
#
# Keep the secrets below in step with apps/api/.env. They are two files that
# must agree; nothing enforces it.
#
# ── Every variable here is REQUIRED, and every secret is EMPTY ─────────────
#
# The Compose file used to carry a literal fallback password for each of these,
# published in tracked source, so a stack started without this file came up
# *successfully* on a password anyone reading the repository already knew. They
# are all `${VAR:?…}` now: Compose refuses to start and names the missing
# variable (AUD2-022).
#
# That only works if the value is genuinely absent. `:?` fires on unset OR
# empty — and NOT on a placeholder string, which is why every secret below is
# left empty rather than filled with something you are trusted to replace.
# An empty value here is a stack that will not start; a placeholder was a stack
# that started on a known password.
#
#   npm run env:init      → copies this file to .env with every blank filled
#                           (24 random bytes as hex, one per secret; refuses to
#                           overwrite an existing .env)
#
# `scripts/env/generate-secrets.mjs` is that script, and it needs no list of
# key names: an empty value in this file is the definition of a secret. Add a
# new one the same way — name it, leave it empty, document it.

# ── Platform Postgres (shared) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Must match DB_USER / DB_PASSWORD / DB_NAME in apps/api/.env.
POSTGRES_USER=postgres
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=kartseek_db

# ── Redis ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Must match REDIS_PASSWORD in apps/api/.env.
REDIS_PASSWORD=

# ── Elasticsearch ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Authentication is ON (AUD2-075): the search index is a denormalised copy of
# the whole catalogue, and it used to be readable, writable and deletable by
# anything that could reach port 9200.
#
# This bootstraps the built-in `elastic` superuser when the security index is
# first created — changing it later does NOT rotate the password; use the
# _security/user API or recreate the `elasticsearch_data` volume.
#
# search-service reads ELASTICSEARCH_NODE from apps/api/.env. Give it the
# credentials there, as userinfo in the URL:
#   ELASTICSEARCH_NODE=http://elastic:<ELASTIC_PASSWORD>@localhost:9200
# and in a container (compose.services.yml), the service name instead:
#   ELASTICSEARCH_NODE=http://elastic:<ELASTIC_PASSWORD>@elasticsearch:9200
ELASTIC_PASSWORD=

# Kibana (the `tools` profile) cannot log in as `elastic` — it refuses that
# account at startup — so it uses the built-in `kibana_system`. That account has
# no bootstrap variable; set its password once, after the cluster is up:
#
#   docker compose exec elasticsearch curl -sS -u elastic:"$ELASTIC_PASSWORD" \
#     -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
#     http://localhost:9200/_security/user/kibana_system/_password \
#     -d "{\"password\":\"$KIBANA_SYSTEM_PASSWORD\"}"
KIBANA_SYSTEM_PASSWORD=

# ── Per-module Postgres credentials ────────────────────────────────────────
# One credential pair per module, used by BOTH topologies:
#
#   • `docker compose --profile isolated up -d` — each pair is that dedicated
#     instance's own superuser. Baked into the volume on first start: changing
#     a password here does NOT change an existing database — you must recreate
#     that volume (`docker compose down -v` drops data) or ALTER the role.
#   • the shared instance — `infra/postgres/init-roles.sh` creates the same
#     eight logins as ordinary roles with rights to their own schema only
#     (AUD2-073), so a module can be moved onto its own credential without the
#     credential itself changing.
#
# REQUIRED for the shared Postgres to start: the roles script exits non-zero,
# naming the variable, rather than creating a role with a default password.
MARKETPLACE_DB_USER=marketplace_user
MARKETPLACE_DB_PASSWORD=
MARKETPLACE_DB_NAME=kartseek_marketplace
GROCERY_DB_USER=grocery_user
GROCERY_DB_PASSWORD=
GROCERY_DB_NAME=kartseek_grocery
RESTAURANT_DB_USER=restaurant_user
RESTAURANT_DB_PASSWORD=
RESTAURANT_DB_NAME=kartseek_restaurant
PHARMACY_DB_USER=pharmacy_user
PHARMACY_DB_PASSWORD=
PHARMACY_DB_NAME=kartseek_pharmacy
DOCTOR_DB_USER=doctor_user
DOCTOR_DB_PASSWORD=
DOCTOR_DB_NAME=kartseek_doctor
HOTEL_DB_USER=hotel_user
HOTEL_DB_PASSWORD=
HOTEL_DB_NAME=kartseek_hotel
TAXI_DB_USER=taxi_user
TAXI_DB_PASSWORD=
TAXI_DB_NAME=kartseek_taxi
FRANCHISE_DB_USER=franchise_user
FRANCHISE_DB_PASSWORD=
FRANCHISE_DB_NAME=kartseek_franchise

# ── Mongo / pgAdmin (tools profile) ────────────────────────────────────────
MONGO_ROOT_USER=admin
MONGO_ROOT_PASSWORD=
MONGO_DB_NAME=kartseek_catalog
PGADMIN_EMAIL=admin@kartseek.com
PGADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ── Platform secrets (the container stack) ─────────────────────────────────
# Read by the generated infra/docker/compose.services.yml, which puts them into
# every API container's `environment:` — where they beat anything apps/api/.env
# says, because those containers run NODE_ENV=production and the gateway's own
# Joi schema then refuses a development secret outright:
#
#   * JWT_SECRET is rejected if it contains dev, test, change, example or
#     placeholder (env.validation.ts). A hex value can contain none of them.
#   * ENCRYPTION_KEY must be exactly 64 hex characters — AES-256-GCM's 32-byte
#     key. `npm run env:init` sizes this one specially.
#
# Both are `${VAR:?…}` in the generated file, so Compose refuses to start and
# names the variable rather than letting the gateway throw at boot.
#
# The host dev fleet (`npm run dev`) does NOT read these: it reads
# apps/api/.env, where NODE_ENV=development and a development secret is fine.
#
# `resolveJwtSecret()` (libs/security/src/jwt.strategy.ts) refuses an unset or
# under-32-character JWT_SECRET in every environment, not only when NODE_ENV
# says production — which is the point, since nothing here sets NODE_ENV. The
# container stack must therefore have a real value in this file; only the host
# dev fleet has the ALLOW_DEV_JWT_SECRET=true escape, in apps/api/.env.
JWT_SECRET=
ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ── Host binding (docker compose) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Which host interface the containers publish on. Loopback for both by default,
# because the gateway behind them may be running with DEV_AUTH_BYPASS=true,
# which treats every anonymous caller as the bypass role — and the datastores
# behind *that* hold the whole platform's data.
#
# NGINX_BIND covers the edge (80/443). DB_BIND covers every datastore port:
# Postgres 5432 and the eight dedicated instances on 5433-5440, Redis 6379,
# Kafka 9092/9101, Mongo 27017, Elasticsearch 9200 (AUD2-140). APP_BIND covers
# the 65 application ports in compose.services.yml — the gateway on 3001, the
# console on 3000, and every service's HTTP, TCP and gRPC port.
#
# Set any of them to 0.0.0.0 to reach the stack from another machine — testing
# the storefront from a phone on the same network is the usual reason — and
# switch it back afterwards. Do not put a datastore on 0.0.0.0 without one.
NGINX_BIND=127.0.0.1
DB_BIND=127.0.0.1
APP_BIND=127.0.0.1
